    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/angeo-26-361-2008",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Solar activity can influence the Earth's environment, and in particular the ozone layer, by direct modulation of the e.m. radiation or through variability of the incoming cosmic ray flux (solar and galactic particles). In particular, solar energetic particles (SEPs) provide additional external energy to the terrestrial environment; they are able to interact with the minor constituents of the atmospheric layer and produce ionizations, dissociations, dissociative ionizations and excitations. This paper highlights the SEP effects on the chemistry of the upper atmosphere by analysing some SEP events recorded during 2005 in the descending phase of the current solar cycle. It is shown that these events can lead to short- (hours) and medium- (days) term ozone variations through catalytic cycles (e.g. HOx and NOx increases). We focus attention on the relationship between ozone and OH data (retrieved from MLS EOS AURA) for four SEP events: 17 and 20 January, 15 May and 8 September. We confirm that SEP effects are different on the night and day hemispheres at high latitudes.",
    "field": "Solar particle effects on polar atmospheric chemistry",
    "problem": "Validating models of atmospheric response to solar particles has been difficult due to a lack of observational data for odd hydrogen species (HOx), which prevented verification of their predicted role in short-term ozone depletion, particularly in the night-time polar atmosphere.",
    "method": "This paper investigates the effects of solar energetic particles on the polar atmosphere by analyzing and correlating satellite-retrieved ozone and hydroxyl radical data with solar proton flux measurements during four specific events in 2005, comparing responses in the day and night hemispheres.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze ozone and hydroxyl (OH) satellite data to investigate the chemical effects of four 2005 Solar Energetic Particle events on the polar upper atmosphere, confirming different impacts on the day and night hemispheres.",
